I was kind of managing a team to work on the project.

00:02:56.910 --> 00:03:01.390
It was lucky, quite lucky, we were quite strong.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:08.470
And at the end, we actually got the number one place, overwhelming number one place in

00:03:08.470 --> 00:03:09.470
the context.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.590
So you were the first ever in the world ImageNet competition with?

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:13.590
Right, right, right.

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.590
Yeah.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:18.230
And I was the president, I did the presentation at the workshop.

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.510
So it was really nice experience for me.

00:03:21.510 --> 00:03:28.990
And that actually gave me into this really large scale computer vision tasks.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:34.630
And so I have been working on this really large scale problem since then.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:43.870
And so when this New York Cat pavement, New York Times Cat came out and also later on

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:46.950
like this AlexNet came out, it really broke my mind.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:52.390
I thought, wow, deep learning is so powerful.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:58.430
And I think since then, I've lost my effort, I think, to work on those.

00:03:58.430 --> 00:04:03.310
So as a head of China's National Lab, National Research Lab on Deep Learning, there must be

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:06.110
a lot of exciting activities going on there.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:10.630
So for the global audience watching this, what should they know about what's happening

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:12.190
with this National Lab?

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:19.190
The mission of this National Engineering Lab is to build a really large deep learning platform.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:24.030
And probably hope to be the biggest one, at least the biggest one in China.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:30.350
And on this platform, we would offer people like a deep learning framework like PADO, PADO.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.830
And we offer people the really large scale computing resource.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:40.950
And we also offer people very large, like very big kind of data.

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:46.990
And if people are able to develop or research or develop a kind of good technology on this

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:50.790
platform, we also offer them like big applications.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:55.750
So for example, the technology can be plugged into some big applications in PADO so that

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.270
the technology could get iterated and improved.

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:07.230
So we believe that combining those with this kind of resource altogether, I think this

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.350
is going to be a really powerful platform.

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:12.790
Let me also give you one example on this.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:19.030
For example, right now, if we publish a paper, let's say a research or publish a paper,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:25.830
if someone wants to reproduce it, the best thing could be probably the person who provides

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:30.830
the code somewhere and you could download the code to your computer.

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:33.630
And you also try to find the data set somewhere.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:40.510
And you probably also need to get the good compilation of your computing resources to

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.590
run smoothly.

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:46.510
So this easily kind of takes some effort.

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:51.270
At the LASER National Lab, things will become much easier.

00:05:51.270 --> 00:05:56.670
So if someone is using this platform to write the paper, to do the work and write the paper,

00:05:56.670 --> 00:06:03.470
and they will have the code on this platform, and the computing structure is already set

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:06.470
up for this code, and the data is led to.

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.910
So basically, you just need a common line to reproduce the result.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:18.070
So this is a huge, I think it's a big, big kind of relief for lots of reproducibility

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:19.910
issues in computer science.

00:06:19.910 --> 00:06:26.630
So easily, just a few seconds, you start learning something like you see in the paper.

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:27.910
So this is truly powerful.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:35.750
So this is just one example that we are working on, to make sure that we are providing really

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:39.670
powerful platform to the community and to the industry.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:40.670
That's amazing.

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:43.350
That really speeds up deep learning research.

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:44.350
Yeah.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:51.110
And then can you give a sense of how much measles the Chinese government is putting

00:06:51.110 --> 00:06:54.030
to back you for this deep learning national lab?

00:06:54.030 --> 00:07:00.270
So I think for Lise, National Engineering Lab, I think the government is going to invest

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:04.430
some funding into here to build up the infrastructure.

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:11.510
But I think more importantly would be, Lise is going to be a flagship in China that is

00:07:11.510 --> 00:07:20.510
going to lead lots of deep learning efforts, including a national project and lots of policies

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:21.510
and things.

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.150
So Lise actually is truly powerful.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:29.110
And I think of what Baidu, we are really honored to get Lise's lab.

00:07:29.110 --> 00:07:32.310
You're somewhat at the heart of deep learning in China.

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:37.430
So there's a lot of activity in China that the global audience isn't aware of yet and

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:38.790
hasn't seen yet.

00:07:38.790 --> 00:07:44.190
So what should people outside China know about deep learning in China?

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:51.470
Yeah, I think in China, especially in the past few years, I think

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:58.150
deep learning powered product service is really booming.

00:07:58.150 --> 00:08:08.790
Coming ranging from search engines to face recognition to surveillance to e-commerce,

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:15.750
lots of places, I think they are investing big effort in the deep learning and also they

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:23.350
are really making use of those technologies to make the business much more powerful.

00:08:23.350 --> 00:08:33.230
And Lise actually is very important for developing AI technology in general.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:38.950
I think for myself and also lots of people share this, we believe that actually it's

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:43.150
very important to have Lise, which is often called positive loop.

00:08:43.150 --> 00:08:50.270
For example, when we start out to think of building some technology, we will have some

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.990
initial data and we try to develop some initial algorithm.

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.750
We launch some initial product for our service.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.790
Then the other day we will get the data from users.

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:09.430
And the other day we get more data and we will develop better algorithms.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:13.030
Because we see more data, we know there will be a better algorithm.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:18.510
So we have more data and better algorithms, we are able to have better technology for the

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:19.510
product service.

00:09:19.510 --> 00:09:24.670
Then definitely we hope to be able to attract more users because the product, the technology

00:09:24.670 --> 00:09:26.990
is better and we will get more data.

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:35.030
So Lise is a very good positive loop and it's very special actually for AI related technologies.

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:41.590
For traditional technology like laser, like laser that I was working on before.

00:09:41.590 --> 00:09:46.270
So it's more like the growth of the technology will be more linear.

00:09:46.270 --> 00:09:51.750
But for this AI technology, because of this positive loop, you can imagine that definitely

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:56.710
at a certain point you can with very fast growth of the technology.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:10:04.270
And Lise is actually super important when we design the research and when we design

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:06.430
our R&D.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:14.190
We work on the direction that we are able to get to this quick improving period.

00:10:14.190 --> 00:10:25.390
If our business is not able to find this positive loop, or if we are not able to find this

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:30.670
strong positive loop, then this direction probably should not work out.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:34.830
Because someone else who has a strong business to find this strong loop and they will get

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:38.870
to this kind of phase much more quickly than you are.

00:10:38.870 --> 00:10:44.910
So Lise is actually a very important logic for us when we are looking at it like I said

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:48.870
hey, in this company what direction we should work on and what direction should not work

00:10:48.870 --> 00:10:49.870
on.

00:10:49.870 --> 00:10:52.070
This definitely is a very important factor we should look at.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:57.670
Today both in China and in the US and globally there are a lot of people wanting to enter

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:00.790
deep learning and wanting to enter AI.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:03.870
What advice would you have for someone that wants to get into this field?

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:11.510
So nowadays definitely I think people who start with open source frameworks are less

00:11:11.510 --> 00:11:16.150
truly powerful to many startups.

00:11:16.150 --> 00:11:23.590
I think when I was studying my deep learning study, there was not much open source resource.

00:11:23.590 --> 00:11:31.230
I think nowadays in AI, especially in deep learning, there is a very good community outlet

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:38.230
and there are multiple, very good deep learning frameworks to start with like TensorFlow,

00:11:38.230 --> 00:11:43.230
Caffe, now we call Caffe2.

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:46.230
In China we have very good Paddle Paddle.

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:56.590
Even for most of those online, they have lots of causes to teach you how to use those.

00:11:56.590 --> 00:12:04.590
And nowadays there are many publicly available benchmarks and people could see really kind

00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:09.590
of skillful, really experienced people, like how well they could do on those benchmarks.

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:13.430
So basically, I took to get familiar with deep learning.

00:12:13.430 --> 00:12:15.910
I think those are very good starting points.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:18.430
How did you gain that understanding?

00:12:18.430 --> 00:12:20.750
So actually I do it in an opposite way.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:29.070
I kind of learned, I learned TCA, I learned LDA, all those, like before I learned deep learning

00:12:29.070 --> 00:12:30.070
actually.

00:12:30.070 --> 00:12:36.790
So basically, that also is a good way I feel.

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:40.230
We kind of lay down lots of foundations.

00:12:40.230 --> 00:12:42.070
We learn graphical model.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:43.670
So these are all kind of important.

00:12:43.670 --> 00:12:47.710
All right now, deep learning is very dominant.

00:12:47.710 --> 00:12:53.790
But knowing those, I should give you very good intuition about how deep learning works.

00:12:53.790 --> 00:13:00.990
And one day probably there's a connection of deep learning to those, like a framework or

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:01.990
approach.

00:13:01.990 --> 00:13:04.310
I think there's already lots of connections.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:07.550
And the loads actually make deep learning richer.

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:12.350
I mean, there are richer ways of doing deep learning.

00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:18.550
So I feel like it's good to start with loads, kind of open source platforms.

00:13:18.550 --> 00:13:21.950
Loads are definitely extremely powerful resource.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:27.790
But meanwhile, I would suggest that you also do learn those basic things about machine learning.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:28.790
So thank you.

00:13:28.790 --> 00:13:29.790
That was fascinating.

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:32.310
Even though I've known you for a long time, there are a lot of details you're thinking

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:33.790
of that I didn't realize until now.

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:34.790
So thank you very much.

00:13:34.790 --> 00:13:36.190
Thank you so much for having me.
